nd him  from what they called a mob mentality I  think that was a case that really  highlighted the danger and the  challenges attendant to mob mentality  Christina Zoopla and Susan stone have  taken on what is for many a very  unpopular set of clients where first and  foremost two females in a me-too world  representing mostly males accused of sex  issues and they won in court in  Youngstown arguing the school was wrong  to apply federal law called title nine  which they said did not cover a case  that occurred before a student was at  the University he was a good student  there were absolutely no conduct  violations this young student is a  wonderful human being who's really  learned from his experience and did not  deserve to be punished twice but it took  going to court going to court and now  that's what they're doing around the  country fighting back against schools  they say often don't know what they're  doing as they try to deal with  allegations of sexual abuse and assault  they become many courts Brian and I mean  we have called a lot of them kangaroo  courts because let's be fair these are  people who make up college body the  faculty the administration even the IT  person can take part in the  investigation and the adjudication of  the process for example they took the  College of Charleston to court to defend  a student who had been cleared by law  enforcement but will still be  investigated of sexual assault by the  college authorities I think no school  wants to be viewed as not taking sexual  assault seriously and so colleges and  universities are again wrestling with  this tension between fairness but also  taking a strong position  to address what is a scourge the issue  was highlighted during the Supreme Court  confirmation hearings for Brett  Kavanaugh accused of sexual assault when  he was an undergrad at Yale allegations  he strongly denied well if the same  thing happened today at Yale Yale  probably would issue an investigation  commenced an investigation into justice  Cavanaugh and he would be facing not  only just an investigation but a  suspension or potential expulsion from  school or worse schools issue no contact  orders an investigation unfolds it's not  unusual at all for students who have  been accused before an investigation has  even begun to be asked to move out of a  dorm to have their class schedule  juggled and in reorganized to avoid  running into an accuser at a certain  point on campus so just as Cavanaugh's  life would essentially be ruined even  before an investigation would be  commenced yet the issue of sex assaults  on campuses including rape is clearly a  scourge that has not been addressed  fully one recent survey found that one  in five female students in their fourth  year of college had experienced  attempted or completed sexual assault  while in college and while 65% of  surveyed rape victims reported what  happened to their friends or family less  than 10 percent reported it to police or  school officials but these two lawyers  say the pendulum has now swing too far  with a lack of fairness and due process  and a number of unintended consequences  we've wrestled with students calling our  saying that they're so depressed and  again that comes back to that approach  of treating our students with compassion  and care you've had clients who are  suicidal yes absolutely many because  because they've lost their friends  they're kicked out of their fraternity  they're kicked out of their sports teams  nobody wants to talk to them nobody  wants to study with them and they feel  such shame after the allegation and such  loneliness and such despair that they  can't see  yonder better future a title nine  finding on a student's record is really  the modern-day scarlet letter to get a  different perspective on this I talked  this week with one of the lawyers who's  been a leader in helping women stand up  to men who they say have abused them  Jeannie Harrison has her own former Los  